<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/minerals-and-crystals">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson introduces the alphabet of geology: minerals — the substances every rock is
    built from. You need nothing from earlier lessons except a willingness to think about
    atoms arranging themselves; everything else is built here, step by step. By the end,
    a pocket knife, a copper coin and an unglazed tile will let you name what you are holding.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state the five-part definition of a mineral and apply each test to a candidate;</li>
    <li>explain how the same chemistry can build different minerals (polymorphs), and why arrangement matters as much as recipe;</li>
    <li>describe the four main ways minerals form, and predict crystal size from cooling rate;</li>
    <li>build the silicate family from its single building block — the silicon–oxygen tetrahedron — from isolated tetrahedra to frameworks;</li>
    <li>name the non-silicate classes and say why crustal minorities carry most of the economy;</li>
    <li>identify a mineral with hardness, cleavage, lustre, streak and density — and know what each test actually measures.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the page: a mineral is a <em>recipe plus an arrangement</em>.
    Neither alone is enough — which is why the definition has teeth, and why two minerals
    of identical chemistry can be the hardest and softest substances on Earth.
  </p>

  <h2>What counts as a mineral</h2>
  <p>
    Pick up any rock and you are holding a handful of minerals — and a mineral is one of the
    most precisely defined objects in natural science: <strong>nature's chemical compounds
    with a structural discipline</strong>. Not "a pretty stone", but a substance that passes
    five tests: it occurs <strong>naturally</strong> (lab-grown rubies are not minerals), it
    is <strong>inorganic</strong>, it is <strong>solid</strong>, its atoms are arranged in an
    ordered, repeating <strong>crystalline lattice</strong>, and it has a
    <strong>definite chemical composition</strong> (or one varying within defined limits).
    Those five criteria do real work. Coal fails (no order in its atoms); glass fails (a
    frozen liquid, no lattice); petroleum fails (liquid, mixed); a lab diamond fails the
    first test — even though it is chemically identical to the mineral. Even ice is a
    mineral only when nature froze it: a glacier's crystals qualify, an ice-cube tray's do
    not. And the definition has graceful edge cases: <strong>opal</strong> is a
    "mineraloid" — natural and solid but without an ordered lattice — while a pearl fails
    because it can only be made by organisms.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="run the five tests — file each candidate" widget="sort-buckets" config={mineralFiveSort} />
  <p>
    Why does a geologist care so much about the ingredients of rocks? Because minerals are
    the alphabet: every rock is a sentence built from them, every mountain range a library,
    and every metal in your phone — copper for wires, lithium for the battery, indium for
    the screen — was once a mineral concentration that had to be found, understood and
    mined. This lesson covers the syllabus's five topics: the chemistry, the formation, the
    great silicate family, the non-silicates, and the identification tests that turn a hand
    specimen into a name. The widget below compresses them to four steps.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the recipe, the lattice, the diagnostic tests, the families" widget="equationStep" config={mineralCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step 1 is the definition doing its filtering work; step
    2 adds the physics of growth — the same lattice story told at two scales, the internal
    order and the outward crystal shape; step 3 is the practical toolkit, which this page
    demonstrates in full at the end; step 4 previews the family tree, which occupies the
    middle of the lesson. The steps build on each other: you cannot use the diagnostic
    tests (step 3) intelligently until you understand why they work — which is the lattice
    of step 2.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a mineral is a naturally occurring, inorganic, crystalline
    solid with a definite composition — and the crystalline part is the deepest requirement.
    Next we see what the lattice itself explains.
  </p>

  <h2>The chemistry of minerals</h2>
  <p>
    The lattice is the heart of the definition, and it explains the subject's most famous
    example. <strong>Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon</strong> — same element,
    different <em>arrangement</em> — and the arrangement changes everything. Diamond bonds
    each carbon to four neighbours in a rigid 3-D framework, the hardest natural substance
    (Mohs 10) and an electrical insulator. Graphite bonds carbons in flat sheets held to the
    next sheet by weak forces: it peels onto paper (pencil "lead"), slides as a lubricant,
    and conducts electricity along its sheets (Mohs 1–2). Minerals with the same chemistry
    but different structure are called <strong>polymorphs</strong>, and the phenomenon runs
    deep — quartz has several pressure-dependent polymorphs used to reconstruct how deep a
    rock was buried.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="diamond vs graphite — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={mineralPolyCompare} />
  <p>
    Two chemical ideas make the rest of the subject click. First, <strong>bonds differ in
    strength</strong>: within a strongly-bonded building block, atoms hold tight, while
    between blocks the bonds are weaker — and a crystal breaks along the weak links, which
    is exactly what <em>cleavage</em> (later in this lesson) measures. Second,
    <strong>substitution is allowed when atoms are similar</strong>: olivine's formula is
    (Mg,Fe)₂SiO₄ — magnesium and iron swap freely in the same lattice, and the swap records
    the temperature of formation. Roughly 6,000 mineral species are named, each a specific
    structure-chemistry pairing, and the International Mineralogical Association polices
    the list — a new species claim needs a crystal structure solution, not just a pretty
    crystal.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "diamonds are rare." Carbon is abundant and diamond
  forms wherever carbon meets the right pressure and temperature; what is scarce is
  <em>gem-quality, mineable concentration</em> — and industrial-grade diamond is cheap
  by the kilogram, with synthetic gemstones now matching mined ones atom for atom. The
  price has always reflected a controlled supply chain as much as geology, which is
  why the crystal in a cutting tool and the one in an engagement ring are the same
  mineral at wildly different markups.</p>

  <h2>How minerals form</h2>
  <p>
    Crystals grow whenever three things coincide: the right elements in the right
    proportions, favourable conditions (temperature, pressure, water, acidity), and
    <em>time</em> — because it takes time for atoms to find their ordered places. Water
    helps, by letting ions travel; time decides size. There are four main birthplaces.
    <strong>From a melt:</strong> as magma cools, minerals crystallise in sequence — olivine
    first (around 1,200 °C), quartz last — and the <strong>cooling rate sets the crystal
    size</strong>: slow cooling deep underground gives atoms time to stack into large,
    well-formed crystals (granite's grains); fast cooling at the surface freezes
    microscopic ones (basalt's); cooling in seconds freezes none at all — glass fails the
    lattice test, which is exactly why obsidian is not a mineral. <strong>From
    solution:</strong> as water evaporates or cools, dissolved ions precipitate — halite
    and gypsum in evaporating basins, cave calcite from dripping carbonate water.
    <strong>From hot fluids:</strong> hydrothermal waters percolating through cracks deposit
    quartz veins and metal ores (much of the world's copper, gold and silver).
    <strong>By transformation:</strong> existing minerals recrystallise under heat and
    pressure — limestone becomes marble, shale's clays become mica — without melting, the
    mechanism of the metamorphic world.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each birthplace with its product" widget="match" config={mineralBirthMatch} />
  <p>
    You can demonstrate the time rule on a windowsill. Dissolve a spoonful of table salt in
    a little hot water and let the bowl stand undisturbed for three days: the ions have
    days to find their places, and you get visible, well-shaped <em>cubes</em>. Boil the
    same solution dry in ten minutes and you get a crust of crystals too small to show any
    shape at all. Same recipe, same lattice, different time — different crystal size.
    Organisms, by the way, grow minerals too: shells are calcite and bones and teeth are
    apatite, and these still count as minerals because chemistry can also make them without
    life — which is the one graceful exception to the "inorganic" test.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "anything crystalline is a mineral." The five tests
  bite harder than intuition expects: sugar grows beautiful crystals but is organic
  (fails the inorganic test), a laboratory-grown alum crystal is flawless but not
  naturally occurring, and petroleum is natural but liquid and unstructured. "Mineral"
  is a status the whole definition must earn.</p>

  <h2>Crystal form: the shape of the lattice</h2>
  <p>
    When a crystal grows unimpeded, its outward faces take on shapes from a limited
    menu: <strong>seven crystal systems</strong> — cubic (halite, pyrite, garnet),
    tetragonal, orthorhombic, monoclinic, triclinic, hexagonal (quartz's six-sided
    prisms) and trigonal (calcite's rhombs) — each a distinct symmetry of the repeating
    lattice. The internal order explains the external habit: halite's cubic cleavage
    and cubic crystals are the same cubic lattice seen at two scales; pyrite grows
    natural cubes; quartz's hexagonal columns with slanted terminations are its
    framework's fingerprint. Habit varies with growth conditions — the same calcite can
    form rhombs, needles or scalenohedra ("dogtooth") — so form is a clue, not an ID
    card; the lattice is the invariant, and X-ray diffraction (Going deeper) is what
    reads it directly.
  </p>

  <h2>The silicates: Earth's default mineral</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Silicates make up more than 90% of the crust</strong> — unsurprisingly, since
    oxygen is the most abundant element in the crust and silicon the second — and they are
    all variations on one building block: the silicon–oxygen <strong>tetrahedron</strong>
    (SiO₄⁴⁻), a pyramid of one silicon and four oxygens, bonded strongly together inside.
    Everything else is architecture — how the tetrahedra link, sharing oxygens.
    <strong>Isolated</strong> tetrahedra bonded by iron and magnesium give
    <strong>olivine</strong>, dense and green, the dominant mineral of the upper mantle.
    <strong>Single chains</strong> give <strong>pyroxene</strong>; <strong>double
    chains</strong> give <strong>amphibole</strong> (the more linkage, the more oxygen
    charges to balance, so calcium, sodium and aluminium join in — complexity increases
    with linkage). <strong>Sheets</strong> — tetrahedra tiled into planes — give the
    <strong>micas</strong> (muscovite, biotite): perfect single-sheet cleavage, the mineral
    that peels into transparent windows. <strong>Frameworks</strong> — every oxygen shared
    between tetrahedra — give <strong>feldspar</strong> (the most abundant mineral family
    in the crust, its pink and white grains in granite) and <strong>quartz</strong>, the
    pure-SiO₂ end member: no charge left to balance, nothing extra to bond, chemically
    stubborn — which is why quartz survives weathering and ends up as every beach sand,
    while the iron–magnesium minerals (olivine, pyroxene, biotite) weather away fast and
    are rare in sediments. The whole sequence is also a thermometer: Bowen's reaction
    series orders it by crystallisation temperature, so a rock's mineral lineup tells you
    the story of its cooling.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="stack the tetrahedron architectures by linkage" widget="order" config={mineralTetraOrder} />

  <h2>The non-silicates: small in mass, mighty in value</h2>
  <p>
    The other mineral classes are crustal minorities but carry most of the economy.
    <strong>Carbonates</strong> (calcite CaCO₃, dolomite) build limestone, marble and most
    shells; calcite's party trick — fizzing with dilute acid — is the standard field test.
    <strong>Oxides</strong> (hematite Fe₂O₃, magnetite Fe₃O₄, rutile TiO₂) are the iron
    and titanium ores: hematite's rust-red streak paints every old mine trail, magnetite
    is the natural magnet. <strong>Sulfides</strong> (pyrite FeS₂ — "fool's gold" —
    galena PbS, chalcopyrite CuFeS₂) carry copper, lead, zinc, silver: metallic, dense,
    and formed where hot fluids met reducing conditions. <strong>Halides</strong>
    (halite = table salt) and <strong>sulfates</strong> (gypsum of plaster and drywall)
    are the evaporite salts. <strong>Native elements</strong> — gold, silver, copper,
    sulfur, carbon (as diamond or graphite) — occur uncombined, and gold's native form is
    precisely why it was humanity's first metal: you can find it shining in a streambed,
    already pure. Note the pattern for economic geology: <strong>ores are unusual mineral
    concentrations</strong> — banded iron formations are fossilised ancient ocean
    chemistry, porphyry coppers are hydrothermal plumbing systems, and the lithium that
    charges your devices comes mostly from evaporated salt lakes (salars) in the Andes,
    where brines concentrated lithium over millennia. Every battery and wind turbine
    starts as one of these.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the faulty line — the valuable minorities" widget="spot-error" config={mineralOreSpot} />

  <h2>A mineral is not a rock</h2>
  <p>
    One distinction separates beginners from students of the subject, and it is worth its
    own heading. A <strong>mineral</strong> is a pure substance with a specific composition
    and structure; a <strong>rock</strong> is a mixture of minerals (a few rocks are
    single-mineral exceptions). Granite, for example, is not a mineral — it is an assembly
    of quartz, feldspar and mica crystals you can learn to point at on a fresh surface:
    the glassy grey grains, the dull white or pink grains, the flaky black specks. Confusing
    the two is famously the commonest mistake in introductory geology, and the examiners
    know it: when a question asks for a rock, a mineral name is wrong, and vice versa.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="mineral or rock? walk the naming decision" widget="branch" config={mineralRockBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "rocks and minerals are the same thing at different
  sizes." It seems reasonable — rocks are made of minerals, and hand specimens of both sit
  in the same drawer. But the relationship is ingredient-to-dish, not big-to-small: a
  mineral is a substance with one recipe and one lattice; a rock is an aggregate that can
  be crushed back into its separate minerals. Halite is a mineral; rock salt <em>deposit</em>
  is a rock made of it; granite is a rock, and no single name covers all of it.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the families are mapped — one building block and its
    architectures, plus the valuable minorities — and you can now say what you are holding
    in category terms. What remains is the practical skill the syllabus ends on: picking up
    an unknown specimen and making it surrender a name.
  </p>

  <h2>Identifying minerals: the field toolkit</h2>
  <p>
    No lab required — the diagnostic properties narrow 6,000 candidates to one in a
    handful of tests. <strong>Hardness</strong> runs on the <strong>Mohs scale</strong>
    (1812), a comparative ladder from talc (1) to diamond (10): fingernail about 2.5,
    copper coin about 3.5, steel knife about 5.5, window glass about 5.5, quartz 7.
    Scratching tells you where a specimen sits between reference points — a mineral your
    knife cannot scratch but quartz can is between 5.5 and 7. <strong>Cleavage</strong> is
    how a mineral breaks along its lattice's weak planes: mica in perfect single sheets,
    halite in cubes (three planes at 90°), calcite in rhombs (three planes not at 90°),
    quartz in none — it <em>fractures</em> into curved conchoidal shells instead. Cleavage
    and fracture are separate observations: cleavage is internal architecture; fracture is
    failure. <strong>Lustre</strong> sorts metallic from glassy, pearly, silky, earthy.
    <strong>Streak</strong> — the powder colour when scraped on unglazed porcelain — is
    more reliable than the specimen's surface colour: hematite looks black, silver or red
    but always streaks rust-red. <strong>Density</strong> separates the heavies: galena
    (7.5 g/cm³) feels twice as heavy as quartz (2.65). Special tests finish the job:
    calcite fizzes in dilute acid; magnetite deflects a compass; halite tastes of salt
    (yes, geologists do).
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="fill the blanks — the field toolkit" widget="cloze" config={mineralKitCloze} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "hardest means unbreakable." Hardness resists
  scratching; toughness resists cracking — different properties. Diamond is Mohs 10
  yet brittle, with cleavage planes a sharp knock can exploit: jewellers cut diamonds
  by splitting them along those planes, and a hammer makes short work of one. Knowing
  the cleavage directions is the practical half of knowing the hardness.</p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="bet before the reveal — how a diamond is cut" widget="predict" config={mineralHardPredict} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Apply the five tests to: (a) a glacier's ice, (b) a snowflake-sized lab diamond, (c) obsidian. Which qualify as minerals?</li>
    <li>Diamond and graphite are both carbon. What differs, and how does it produce the opposite properties?</li>
    <li>Your knife cannot scratch an unknown clear mineral, but a quartz crystal can. What range is its hardness, and what family of observations do you try next?</li>
    <li>Why does mica peel into sheets while halite breaks into cubes? Answer at the lattice level.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Glacier ice qualifies: natural, inorganic, solid,
    crystalline, definite composition (H₂O). The lab diamond fails "naturally occurring".
    Obsidian fails "crystalline" — it is glass, cooled too fast for any lattice. (2) The
    <em>arrangement</em>: diamond's rigid 3-D framework versus graphite's weakly-stacked
    sheets — hardness, slipperiness and conductivity all follow from structure, not
    chemistry. (3) Between 5.5 and 7. Next: cleavage or fracture (quartz has none —
    conchoidal fracture), then streak, density, and acid. (4) Mica's tetrahedra are tiled
    into sheets bonded to each other by weak links, so break follows the planes between
    sheets; halite's lattice is symmetric in all three directions, so it breaks equally at
    90° in three planes — cleavage is the weak-bond map of the lattice.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A mineral is naturally occurring, inorganic, solid, crystalline, and definite in composition — all five tests, every time.</li>
    <li>Recipe plus arrangement: polymorphs (diamond/graphite) prove structure matters as much as chemistry.</li>
    <li>Crystals grow given ingredients, conditions and time; cooling rate sets crystal size, and water speeds the ions.</li>
    <li>Minerals form from melts, solutions, hot fluids and transformation — and organisms grow some (shells, teeth) that chemistry can also make.</li>
    <li>Silicates dominate the crust because oxygen and silicon dominate it; their architecture is the shared tetrahedron: isolated, chains, sheets, frameworks.</li>
    <li>Quartz (framework, no leftover charge) weathers last and becomes the beach; ferromagnesian minerals weather first and vanish from sediments.</li>
    <li>Non-silicate classes — carbonates, oxides, sulfides, halides, sulfates, native elements — are crustal minorities that carry the economy.</li>
    <li>Ores are unusual concentrations: hydrothermal veins, banded iron, evaporated brines.</li>
    <li>A rock is a mixture of minerals, not a big mineral — the field's commonest confusion.</li>
    <li>Identification runs on hardness (Mohs), cleavage, lustre, streak and density: a few tests eliminate thousands.</li>
    <li>Hardness resists scratching, not breaking — diamond cleaves.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Minerals are the alphabet; the next lessons write with it. Volcanoes and igneous
    processes use Bowen's series and the cooling-rate rule to explain whole rock types;
    weathering and erosion turn mineral stability (quartz survives, olivine does not) into
    landscapes and sediments; the rock cycle ties melt, sediment and metamorphism into one
    loop. Later, the energy-and-mineral-resources lesson returns to this page's economic
    thread with the ore deposits themselves. Keep the five tests and the tetrahedron in
    your pocket — every later chapter assumes them.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Run the toolkit once, end to end, to see the narrowing. An unknown specimen: glassy
    lustre, colourless, no cleavage but curved conchoidal fracture, scratches glass and
    a steel knife (hardness over 5.5) but not a quartz crystal (under 7), streaks white,
    density 2.65, no fizz in acid. The tests have eliminated all malleable metallic
    minerals, everything softer than 5.5, everything with cleavage, all carbonates (no
    fizz), and the density rules out beryl and close rivals (feldspar's two good cleavages
    are absent). What survives is <strong>quartz</strong> — and the identification took
    four observations. This is the discipline of mineral identification: not memorising
    6,000 species, but knowing the ~30 common rock-formers cold and letting the tests do
    the elimination.
  </p>
  <p>
    Minerals are also <strong>records</strong>. The chemistry and inclusions of a crystal
    record the pressure, temperature and fluids of its growth, and the record can be dated:
    <strong>zircon</strong> (ZrSiO₄) incorporates uranium but rejects lead when it grows, so
    every lead atom inside is radiogenic decay — a clock started at crystallisation. Zircon
    crystals from Western Australia dated at 4.4 billion years are the oldest known material
    from Earth, and their oxygen-isotope chemistry implies liquid water existed startlingly
    early in the Hadean — a planet's entire early history read from a sand-grain-sized
    mineral. The same logic (and the same mineral) dates the oldest lunar and Martian grains.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deeper science is <strong>crystallography</strong>. That the outward habit mirrors
    the inner lattice was guessed for centuries, but proven when von Laue (1912) and the
    Braggs father-and-son team (Nobel 1915) fired X-rays through crystals and read the
    diffraction: crystals are diffraction gratings with atomic spacing. X-ray
    crystallography then became the way matter is seen — the structures of table salt,
    penicillin, insulin, and finally DNA were all solved by shining X-rays through
    crystals — a technique born from geology that restructured chemistry, physics and
    biology in turn. Gems close the loop between science and value:
    diamond (carbon at mantle pressures), ruby (chromium impurity in corundum — the same
    mineral as sapphire, coloured differently), emerald (beryl with chromium or vanadium) —
    value tracks the rarity of the <em>conditions</em>, not just hardness. And the frontier
    keeps moving: "mineral evolution" studies how the mineral kingdom itself grew from
    about a dozen pre-solar species to thousands, as biology — oxygen-producing life above
    all — gave the planet new chemistry to crystallise.
  </p>

